{"name":"Seascape Blue","hex":"#a6bad1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seascape","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A view of the sea.","A piece of art that depicts the sea or shoreline."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seascape"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seascape","blocks":[{"text":"A seascape is a photograph, painting, or other work of art which depicts the sea, in other words an example of marine art. The word originated as a formation from landscape, which was first used for images of land in art. By a similar development, \"seascape\" has also come to mean actual perceptions of the sea itself. It is applied in planning contexts to geographical locations possessing a good view of the sea. Seascape aesthetics receive legal protection in terms of biodiversity/ health of the seas (the OSPAR Convention, and in terms of the visual bio-cultural seascape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/The_Wave_-_Pierre-Auguste_Renoir_-_Google_Cultural_Institute.jpg/330px-The_Wave_-_Pierre-Auguste_Renoir_-_Google_Cultural_Institute.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seascape"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg/500px-Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg/1280px-Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg/500px-Winslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Summer Squall, 1904. A seascape by Winslow Homer.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWinslow_Homer_Summer_Squall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Clifton_Beach_5.jpg/500px-Clifton_Beach_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Clifton_Beach_5.jpg/1280px-Clifton_Beach_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Clifton_Beach_5.jpg/500px-Clifton_Beach_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A seascape photograph at Clifton Beach, South Arm, Tasmania, Australia","link":"https://